hey guys it's Theodore from practical  psychology and today we're going to be  doing an animated book review of the  4-hour workweek for believe nation the  4-hour workweek is written by Timothy  Ferriss now Tim is an awesome guy who  loves getting more results from less  input and he is known for all of his  awesome life hacks imagine running a  business outsourcing your life and  creating a full-time income for only  four hours a week of work while  traveling to exotic places to scuba dive  tango dance and practice MMA Fighting's  this is very possible Tim did it and he  gives us tips in this book so we can  learn how to do it too the first thing  that resonated with me was that Tim says  that most people choose unhappiness over  uncertainty many people would rather  work a nine-to-five job they hate than  have an unreliable paycheck if you're an  entrepreneur you cannot breathe doing  this he also says that many people have  very low goals and because of that they  fail you see when someone sets a really  high goal there is less competition and  you're instantly more likely to win  there are only two things that can  happen wherever you set a high goal  either you succeed or you learn and with  this kind of mentality you will win  basically Tim is telling you this  because most people can find 2 to 4  extra hours in their day to either start  or grow a simple business money machine  that can send them on these awesome  exotic resorts and getaways they put in  the extra work and truly believe that  it's possible most people aren't looking  for a million-dollar house or a Ferrari  or even a free trip to the Bahamas  instead what they're looking for is to  cure their boredom that's where the  4-hour workweek is all about giving you  the freedom to stimulate your mind and  love living your life for me learning is  my drive and offering book reviews like  this on my channel is just a way I can  make a living out of it and make an  impact on people let's talk about  something really important Tim advises  going on the low information diet he  says most of the information we gather  throughout the day falls in four  categories time consuming negative  relevant to your goals or outside of  your influence  watching soap box operas time-consuming  watching the news late at night about a  guy who has stabbed 47 times last month  negative listening to the radio about  bombings in a country halfway across the  globe outside of your influence and also  negative read about that new deadly  virus vaccine irrelevant to your goals  in fact he challenges you to find one  out of 10 things that you watch hear or  read throughout the day that aren't one  of these remove stuff like this from  your life and you'll quickly be on the  right path tim says another big concept  that Tim talks about is automation and  outsourcing I remember when I was  growing up my dad had a friend who had  spent three hours a week trimming and  mowing his yard he was a lawyer mind you  so he could easily earn a hundred  dollars an hour if he worked overtime it  would be so easy for him to hire a high  school student to do the same task for  30 dollars and if he did this he could  work overtime and make a profit of 270  dollars with this time now this is  different if you enjoy doing stuff like  this and if there is value to you of  doing it but he is always complaining  about how you have to dig up weeds and  resprayed his seat  the basic idea here is that you should  spend your time doing things that are  the most value to you and one of the  ways you can do this is by outsourcing  your tasks tim mentions hiring a virtual  assistant before i start talking about  these let me tell you that it can be  very difficult to find a quality VA most  of the time they're overseas and in  different time zones some have trouble  communicating in english the time and  energy you save is definitely worth it  these hired hands can do anything from  web research to writing articles to even  remembering birthdays and automating  Amazon purchases if you trust them  enough the idea here is outsourcing give  low return or repetitive tasks to  someone else for a price evan has a team  and he can do this great he can put  notes together set up the video details  and have his team worry about the rest  of the details this is how he's able to  create so much content so quickly this  is the whole idea of the 4-hour workweek  to outsource your tasks for a profit  whether that be money or meaning or  adding value to someone's else's life  remember that profit is different for  everyone  profit can be 100 thousand dollars a  year profit can be making an impact on  1,000 people's lives profit could be  spending a month vacation with your  family  lastly I'd like to give you a few tips  about product creation Tim made his  quick buck selling cognitive supplements  he sold them to people and had his team  worry about the details he had the  original idea and took the risk to start  the company so he gained the reward when  it succeeded he suggests pricing a  product between 50 and 200 dollars  simply because most of the people that  are willing to pay that amount won't  complain about the small details once  they buy the product and this is proven  through his research you will also have  to spend less and manufacturing  thousands of products opposed to a few  hundred one of the easiest and best ways  to do this is to create a knowledge  product instead of creating a physical  product you can create a knowledge  product like an e-book or a series of  videos in which you can sell to the user  those that do well either teach the end  user something or entertain the user  they get rid of our first problem to end  boredom there is no recurring fee to  create this kind of product and you can  sell it an unlimited amount of times  evan is doing a great job creating all  of these videos simply because they will  last forever the impact he has on  everyone else's lives who watch these  videos will last forever  can you imagine showing your  grandchildren something that you created  unless there is something like a black  hole or a catastrophic internet failure  everything he creates all the content he  creates online will be available forever  one other thing that Tim suggests is  setting up a fake sales page and  allowing potential customers to click  all the way through the buying process  until they get to a screen that says  sorry we're out of stock and using some  handy web tools you can see how many  people would have bought the product if  it was available now the cool thing  about this trick is that you don't  actually have to have a product yet you  can see who would buy your product if  you made it you can even set it up so  that it'll send them an email after you  make the product or when you  quote-unquote restock I'm so glad Evan  gave me the opportunity to speak and  give value to this awesome community I  hope I added value to your life to the  time you have given me and if you want  more awesome book reviews like this  check out my channel at practical  psychology in the description below if  you've ever hired a VA or outsourced to  any task leave a comment below  describing your experience and thanks  for
 

